Following are the generated assertions to correspond to the comments with "@@@" prefix:

```cs
Debug.Assert(true); // @@@ Add operation.
Debug.Assert(arrList.Contains(x)); // @@@ Add operation.
Debug.Assert(arrList.IndexOf(x) != -1); // @@@ Remove operation.
Debug.Assert(index < arrList.Count); // @@@ Insert operation.
Debug.Assert(index < arrList.Count); // @@@ Get operation.
Debug.Assert(arrList.IndexOf(x) != -1); // @@@ IndexOf operation.
Debug.Assert(arrList.Contains(x)); // @@@ Contains operation.
Debug.Assert(index < arrList.Count); // @@@ LastIndexOf operation.
Debug.Assert(index < arrList.Count); // @@@ Set operation.
```

The code above are assertion checks where the operations are performed. `Debug.Assert()` is used to perform runtime assertions and checks in the program.
The generated Assert statements checks for whether the conditions for the corresponding operations still hold after the operation. This gives a way to verify or validate if the operations are still correct.

Note: `Debug.Assert()` can be turned off during release builds for performance improvement.--------------------The following code is a simple extraction of the "@@@ new arrList contains x" comment and the corresponding "arrList.Contains\(x\)" statement:

```cs
