Here is the Python code for this:

```python
import re

# Input code
code = """
@@@ new arry contains x
"""

# Strip comments
lines = re.split('\n', code)
lines = [line.strip() for line in lines]
lines = [line for line in lines if line and not line.startswith("//")]
code = "\n".join(lines)

# Parse code
match = re.match('^@@@ (.*)', code)
assertion = match.group(1)

print(f'Debug.Assert({assertion});')
```

As for the C# code, it's not straightforward because the "@@@" prefix isn't part of the programming language. The assertion is an extra comment and it's displayed as it is, not as part of a C# statement. So, it won't be converted via any parsing, it will be directly echoed from the comments.

If you want this script to parse the original C# code, then you would need to add another parsing step to identify the comments containing "@@@" and extract the assertion from them. This would complicate things a bit.--------------------Here's a sample code snippet showing how to extract the output assertions from your input code using regex in C#.

```cs
using System;
using System.Text.RegularExpressions;

class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        var code = @"
            ...
            // @@@ new arrList contains x
        }
        ...
        ";

        // Regex pattern to match "Debug.Assert(...);"
        var regex = new Regex(@"Debug\.Assert\(.*?\);");

        MatchCollection matches = regex.Matches(code);

        foreach (Match match in matches)
        {
            Console.WriteLine(match.Value);
        }
    }
}
```

Modify the code string `...` to contain the appropriate code snippet you want to parse. Running the Main method will print out all lines of code matching the regex pattern `Debug.Assert(...);` in the console.

Please note that the code can't be run as it's just a hypothetical code, that you would need to replace with the input from the prompt.

Please note that this solution depends on the specific requirements of the input code that you provide. If the input code has a lot of complexity, the matching process could be more complex. If necessary, you can adjust the regex pattern to better match certain parts of the code. You might also need to use non-greedy operators (?) or look-around assertions to handle situations where "Debug.Assert" is part of a larger string. This needs to be tested extensively to correctly adapt to all possible scenarios.--------------------